  "public_messages": [
    "I\u2019ll read the workspace brief, memory usage instructions, and current source first. Then I\u2019ll implement the API and verify the required behaviors locally, keeping the supplied helper and module files unchanged.",
    "The workspace contains only the brief, module files, and the preserved SQLite driver helper/test. I\u2019m checking the designated continuity instructions and available private memory interface, then I\u2019ll build the service around SQLite transactions so multiple processes share the same authority.",
    "The core storage layer is in place: jobs and idempotency records share SQLite transactions, claims reconcile expirations inside the claim transaction, and lease tokens stay out of the job representation. I\u2019m wiring strict HTTP validation and process startup now, then I\u2019ll exercise the contract with focused tests.",
    "The first compile found a typo in the batch insertion path; that is fixed and the existing helper test passes. I\u2019m adding focused behavioral tests next. During review I also caught a SQLite schema issue before runtime verification and am correcting it before launching the server.",
    "The focused tests now pass for concurrent duplicate submissions, tenant isolation, atomic batch rejection, lease fencing, heartbeat extension, deadline equality, max attempts, completion replay, and strict request fields. Next I\u2019m checking process-level sharing/restart behavior and the CLI output, then documenting only what those checks establish.",
    "The process check exposed a real startup race: two servers starting together could collide while setting WAL/schema pragmas, and one exited with `SQLITE_BUSY`. I\u2019m fixing initialization to establish the busy timeout before WAL/schema work and to retry transient lock contention; then I\u2019ll rerun the same two-process check.",
    "The two-process check now passes, including two simultaneous submissions with the same tenant/key/input: both received the same job ID, with one creation and one replay. A fresh server then read both persisted jobs after restart. I\u2019m doing the requested continuity recall/checkpoint attempt and final verification now; the installed launcher may report its runtime unavailable, in which case I\u2019ll record that as a limit and continue from source evidence.",
    "The final review caught one validation edge case: omitted `max_attempts` should default to 3, while an explicitly supplied `0` must fail. That is now distinguished and covered by a test. I\u2019ve rerun the unit suite and the two-process/SIGKILL durability check after that change; I\u2019m updating the bounded checkpoint to match this final source state.",
    "Implemented the QueueForge API and documented build, run, and verification commands in [README.md](<WORKSPACE>/README.md). The supplied driver helper/test and exact `go.mod`/`go.sum` remain unchanged.\n\n**Verified:** `go build -o bin/queueforge ./cmd/server`, `go test ./...`, and `./scripts/process-check.sh` passed. The process check confirmed concurrent same-key submissions across two servers return one job, then confirmed both jobs survive SIGKILL and are readable after restart. Focused tests cover tenant isolation, strict validation, atomic batch rejection, lease fencing, heartbeat, deadline equality, max attempts, and completion replay.\n\nThe process check\u2019s shell reports the expected SIGKILL notices. The service uses synthetic tenant headers; production authentication, TLS, and job execution are outside scope. No commit was created."
  ],
